    "math_academy_id": "q-149570",
    "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
    "problem": "| **Years in the 1st division ($x$)** | $44$ | $38$ | $32$ | $30$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of Championships ($y$)** | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of years four English soccer teams have been in the top division and the number of national championships they have won. \nCompute the covariance of the data sets.",
    "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (44+38+32+30) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 144 \\\\  & =36 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+3+3+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(44\\,-\\,36)(4\\,-\\,4)+(38\\,-\\,36)(3\\,-\\,4)+(32\\,-\\,36)(3\\,-\\,4)+(30\\,-\\,36)(6\\,-\\,4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(8)(0)+(2)(-1)+(-4)(-1)+(-6)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+(-2)+4+(-12)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
